[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m output:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m To find the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins, follow these steps:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m 1. **Understand the Problem**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    - You have seven bags initially, each containing the same number of gold coins, let’s call this number \( n \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    - You find an additional bag with 53 coins.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    - You redistribute all the coins into eight bags so that each bag ends up with the same number of coins.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m 2. **Set Up the Equation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    After redistributing, each of the 8 bags will have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \text{Number of coins in each bag} = \frac{n + 53}{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    Since the number of coins in each bag must be an integer, \( \frac{n + 53}{8} \) must be an integer. Therefore:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    n + 53 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    n + 53 \equiv 0 \pmod{8} \implies n \equiv -53 \pmod{8} \implies n \equiv -53 + 64 \pmod{8} \implies n \equiv 11 \pmod{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    So, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    n \equiv 11 \pmod{8} \implies n \equiv 3 \pmod{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    This tells us that \( n = 8k + 3 \) for some integer \( k \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m 3. **Find the Smallest \( n \) Such That \( n > 200 \)**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    n = 8k + 3 > 200 \implies 8k > 197 \implies k > \frac{197}{8} \implies k > 24.625
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    Since \( k \) must be an integer, the smallest integer greater than 24.625 is 25. Substituting \( k = 25 \) into the equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    n = 8(25) + 3 = 200 + 3 = 203
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    So, the smallest number of coins you could have had initially is **203**. To verify, if \( n = 203 \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \frac{203 + 53}{8} = \frac{256}{8} = 32
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m    Each bag would have 32 coins after redistribution.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88790)[0m Therefore, the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins is \(\boxed{203}\).
